comments.body:
Live geography review follow-up — 2026-10-08

The user is seeing multiple visible conquest-map geography defects during current map testing, including:
- tile boundaries/raster shapes that do not match the intended hand-drawn conquest map;
- apparent river/gap channels that are not actual intended rivers/geographic separations;
- some tiles apparently containing two waypoint markers, which may turn out to be a raster-boundary issue rather than waypoint metadata.

Important design clarification from the user:
The hand-drawn map was never intended to freeze the conquest raster permanently. Boundary/resource corrections are expected as live review finds implementation mistakes. The system must remain practically editable at the source-resource level even though validated runtime snapshots are immutable.

Please explicitly distinguish:
- immutable validated runtime snapshot / stable IDs, which should remain;
from
- editable canonical raster/resource data, whose cell assignments and boundaries may be deliberately corrected after review.

For future correction work:
- compare current raster against the intended hand-drawn source and visible LOTR geography;
- inventory concrete wrong-river/wrong-boundary cases with tile IDs and coordinates;
- classify whether each defect came from the original drawing or from translation/implementation into KOME;
- preserve stable tile IDs and evaluate persisted Build/waypoint/company/conflict reference impact before edits;
- allow deliberate boundary edits rather than treating current cells as untouchable;
- retain explicit exclusions/gaps only where they are actually intended geography.

The user expects meaningful map cleanup work and does not want implementation constraints to turn the current raster into an effectively uneditable final map.
